WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.880
Elena considers a Roth individual retirement arrangement.

2
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.600
Regular Roth contributions are made with after-tax money and are not

3
00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:10.600
deductible. That is different from a traditional contribution that qualifies

4
00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:11.720
for a current deduction.

5
00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:16.240
Roth eligibility still requires the applicable compensation and contribution

6
00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:21.160
rules, and direct regular contributions can be reduced or prohibited by

7
00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:23.820
modified adjusted gross income and filing status.

8
00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:27.460
A high income that prevents a direct Roth contribution is not

9
00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:30.100
the same rule as the traditional deduction phaseout.

10
00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:33.640
Nor does it automatically answer whether a conversion is possible.

11
00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:38.120
Separate regular contributions, transfers, conversions and distributions

12
00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:39.540
before applying a limit.

13
00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:41.820
Those words describe different transactions.

14
00:00:42.745 --> 00:00:46.325
For twenty twenty-six, a person under age fifty generally has a

15
00:00:46.325 --> 00:00:50.585
seven thousand five hundred dollar combined regular contribution limit across

16
00:00:50.585 --> 00:00:55.525
traditional and Roth individual retirement arrangements, subject to

17
00:00:55.525 --> 00:00:57.265
compensation and other restrictions.

18
00:00:58.405 --> 00:01:00.545
It is not a fresh limit for every account.

19
00:01:01.205 --> 00:01:06.465
Suppose Elena is forty, has sufficient compensation, qualifies for

20
00:01:06.465 --> 00:01:10.765
a full direct Roth contribution and has already contributed three thousand

21
00:01:10.765 --> 00:01:13.545
dollars to a traditional account for the year.

22
00:01:14.145 --> 00:01:17.845
She generally has forty-five hundred dollars of that combined limit left.

23
00:01:18.665 --> 00:01:22.105
The age-fifty catch-up increases the general twenty twenty-six dollar limit

24
00:01:22.105 --> 00:01:26.145
by eleven hundred dollars, but does not override a lower compensation

25
00:01:26.145 --> 00:01:28.485
amount or Roth income restriction.

26
00:01:29.259 --> 00:01:33.679
When a Roth distribution is not qualified, the ordering rules matter.

27
00:01:34.479 --> 00:01:38.919
Ordinary distributions reach regular contributions first, then conversions

28
00:01:39.759 --> 00:01:43.539
and applicable rollover amounts under their ordering rules, and earnings

29
00:01:43.539 --> 00:01:47.579
last. Aggregate the owner's Roth arrangements as required;

30
00:01:48.919 --> 00:01:51.299
opening another account does not restart the order.

31
00:01:52.039 --> 00:01:56.299
Suppose Elena has twelve thousand dollars of unrecovered regular Roth

32
00:01:56.299 --> 00:02:00.579
contributions and three thousand dollars of earnings, with no conversions,

33
00:02:01.459 --> 00:02:04.099
earlier distributions or other adjustments.

34
00:02:04.959 --> 00:02:08.179
An ordinary five thousand dollar withdrawal comes from regular contributions

35
00:02:08.179 --> 00:02:13.439
and is not subject to income tax or the usual early-distribution

36
00:02:13.439 --> 00:02:15.299
additional tax under these facts.

37
00:02:16.019 --> 00:02:19.319
Do not treat it as five thousand dollars of earnings merely

38
00:02:19.319 --> 00:02:20.739
because the account has grown.

39
00:02:21.503 --> 00:02:25.583
A qualified Roth distribution can exclude earnings as well as contributions.

40
00:02:26.583 --> 00:02:30.163
It generally must occur after the five-tax-year period starting with the

41
00:02:30.163 --> 00:02:32.923
first year for which a Roth contribution was made for the

42
00:02:32.923 --> 00:02:35.583
owner, and satisfy a qualifying condition.

43
00:02:36.403 --> 00:02:40.383
Those conditions include reaching age fifty-nine and one half, qualifying

44
00:02:40.383 --> 00:02:45.283
disability, death or an eligible first-home payment within its lifetime

45
00:02:45.283 --> 00:02:50.063
limit. Suppose Rosa first contributed for twenty twenty-one and takes a

46
00:02:50.063 --> 00:02:53.403
distribution in twenty twenty-six at age sixty-one.

47
00:02:54.323 --> 00:02:56.723
The stated time and age conditions are met.

48
00:02:57.763 --> 00:03:02.183
Turning sixty-one alone would not satisfy the five-year condition if her

49
00:03:02.183 --> 00:03:05.223
first Roth contribution had only just occurred.

50
00:03:06.155 --> 00:03:10.215
Converting pretax traditional retirement money to a Roth arrangement

51
00:03:10.215 --> 00:03:14.955
generally includes the previously untaxed amount in income for the conversion

52
00:03:14.955 --> 00:03:19.495
year. It is not a deduction and does not use the

53
00:03:19.495 --> 00:03:24.515
regular contribution limit. A separate five-year additional-tax rule

54
00:03:24.515 --> 00:03:28.815
can apply if a younger owner soon withdraws an amount attributable

55
00:03:28.815 --> 00:03:30.735
to a taxable conversion.

56
00:03:31.815 --> 00:03:34.035
Each conversion has its own clock.

57
00:03:34.035 --> 00:03:39.055
For example, an age-forty-five owner converts in

58
00:03:39.055 --> 00:03:43.515
twenty twenty-six and withdraws taxable converted amounts in twenty

59
00:03:43.515 --> 00:03:46.235
twenty-eight with no applicable exception.

60
00:03:47.575 --> 00:03:51.555
An additional tax can apply even though the conversion amount was

61
00:03:51.555 --> 00:03:53.795
already included in income earlier.

62
00:03:54.735 --> 00:03:59.555
Do not confuse this conversion rule with the five-year qualified-distribution

63
00:03:59.555 --> 00:04:04.545
test. A Roth owner does not have lifetime required minimum distributions

64
00:04:04.545 --> 00:04:06.885
from their Roth individual retirement arrangement.

65
00:04:07.425 --> 00:04:11.005
That does not mean beneficiaries can always leave inherited funds untouched

66
00:04:11.005 --> 00:04:15.305
forever. Death-distribution requirements still depend on beneficiary

67
00:04:15.305 --> 00:04:19.585
classification, and the earnings exclusion still requires the applicable

68
00:04:19.585 --> 00:04:22.945
qualification rules. Compare the two arrangements carefully.

69
00:04:23.945 --> 00:04:28.285
Traditional contributions may provide an eligible deduction now, with later

70
00:04:28.285 --> 00:04:30.605
pretax distributions generally taxed.

71
00:04:31.225 --> 00:04:35.165
Roth contributions provide no current deduction, but qualified distributions

72
00:04:35.165 --> 00:04:39.705
can be excluded. Both require attention to contribution rules and the

73
00:04:39.705 --> 00:04:43.825
actual transaction. Neither label by itself establishes a person's best

74
00:04:43.825 --> 00:04:48.885
choice. On the exam, identify the tax treatment
the stated facts actually support.
